public static boolean isValidSudoku(char[][] board) {
  // Initialize a boolean array to keep track of which digits have been used in each row
  boolean[][] rowUsed = new boolean[9][9];

  // Initialize a boolean array to keep track of which digits have been used in each column
  boolean[][] colUsed = new boolean[9][9];

  // Initialize a boolean array to keep track of which digits have been used in each sub-box
  boolean[][] boxUsed = new boolean[9][9];

  for (int row = 0; row < 9; row++) {
    for (int col = 0; col < 9; col++) {
      // If the current cell is not empty, mark the digit as used in the corresponding row, column, and sub-box
      if (board[row][col] != '.') {
        int digit = board[row][col] - '0' - 1;

        if (rowUsed[row][digit] || colUsed[col][digit] || boxUsed[row - (row % 3)][col - (col % 3)][digit]) {
          return false;
        } else {
          rowUsed[row][digit] = true;
          colUsed[col][digit] = true;
          boxUsed[row - (row % 3)][col - (col % 3)][digit] = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return true;
}

The code above uses three boolean arrays: `rowUsed`, `colUsed`, and `boxUsed` to keep track of which digits have been used in each row, column, and sub-box, respectively. It loops through each cell in the board and marks the corresponding digit as used in the row, column, and sub-box if it is not empty. If it is empty, it checks if the digit has already been used in the corresponding row, column, and sub-box. If it has, it returns `false`. If it hasn't, it marks the digit as used in the row, column, and sub-box and moves on to the next cell. If all cells are valid, it returns `true`.

It is important to note that the digits in the top left and bottom left corners are not compared to determine if a board is valid. This is because the 3x3 sub-boxes overlap, and the window of overlap is ignored for the purpose of this question. Therefore, the top left and bottom left corners are not compared.

